Why: /api/auth/reset-password, /api/auth/magic-link/send and /api/auth/resend-verification return the token, OTP and link in the HTTP response, and the UI shows them as buttons. Anyone can take over any account with only an email address. Do an emergency hotfix first: remove those fields from responses, redeploy, invalidate outstanding tokens, check audit logs.
(Paste Prompt 0 first. This is a critical security fix.)

Fix the authentication recovery flows. Currently /api/auth/reset-password, /api/auth/magic-link/send and /api/auth/resend-verification return the token, OTP, magic link URL and direct reset URL in the HTTP response, and the UI offers 'Authenticate & Open Workspace Now', 'Instant Enclave Clearance', 'Copy Link' and 'Copy Code'.

Backend (src/server/authRoutes.ts, authTokenStore.ts):
1. In production NO auth endpoint may ever return: token, resetToken, code, otp_code, preview_code, magic_link, magicLinkUrl, directResetUrl, debugLink, or any secret. Return only {status: 'success', message: 'If an account exists for this email, we sent instructions.'} with the SAME body, status code and similar timing whether or not the account exists. Dev preview is allowed only when NODE_ENV !== 'production' AND ALLOW_DEV_AUTH_PREVIEW === 'true' AND the request comes from localhost.
2. Tokens: 32 random bytes (base64url), store only a SHA-256 hash, bind to email and purpose (signin, signup, reset), single use with atomic consume, 15 minute expiry, and invalidate earlier outstanding tokens of the same purpose when a new one is issued.
3. If a numeric code is kept at all, it is a fallback typed in the same browser: 6 digits, hashed, 10 minute expiry, maximum 5 attempts then lock for 15 minutes, constant-time comparison. Prefer link-only for password reset. Remove the duplicate OTP keys (recovery:, reset:, any:).
4. Magic-link sign-in: at request time set a short-lived HttpOnly, Secure, SameSite=Lax nonce cookie and bind the token to it. Opening the link shows a 'Confirm sign in' page that requires a click and a POST to consume the token, so email scanners that prefetch links cannot consume it. If the cookie is missing (different device), show 'Enter the code from your email' instead of signing in.
5. Password reset: consume token, enforce password policy (minimum 12 characters, breached-password check, reject the email as the password), hash with argon2id or bcrypt cost 12, revoke ALL sessions and refresh tokens, send a 'your password was changed' email with a 'this was not me' link, write an audit log entry.
6. Email verification at signup: same rules; an unverified account cannot sign in or call the API.
7. Rate limits: per email (5 requests/hour) and per IP (20/hour) with Retry-After, plus Cloudflare Turnstile on the request forms. Never reveal in an error message whether an email is registered.
8. If email delivery is not configured in production, fail the readiness check and log an error; never claim a message was dispatched.
9. Remove tokens from the URL after reading them (history.replaceState), send Referrer-Policy: no-referrer on those pages, and make sure tokens and codes never appear in logs or analytics.

Frontend (LoginView.tsx, SignupView.tsx, ForgotPasswordView.tsx, ResetPasswordView.tsx, MagicLinkVerifyView.tsx, AuthModal.tsx):
